// This Pine Script® code is subject to the terms of the Mozilla Public License 2.0 at https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/
// © henry_oliver
// 🐋 BLOCK TRADES PANEL — companion to 📰 Ticker Tape
// ONE plot DRAWS, and only one. Height = how many individual prints ≥ the size
// threshold hit that bar (Barry's "block trades" — single large orders, NOT
// cumulative bar volume). Colour = what those prints were: cyan buy-side / red
// sell-side, two tiers per side for aggressive vs passive, NORD4 for a mid-dominant
// (dark pool) bar and for a dead-even one. The bar carries its whole state: how
// much, which way, how urgent.
//
// The pane stays one column per bar. What CHANGED on 2026-08-21 (this note used to
// say there were no per-type series at all) is that the five buckets behind the
// colour are now published as READOUTS — status line and Data Window, never
// `display.pane`, so they add no marks and no autoscale. A five-block column is 5
// at-ask or 5 hit-the-bid or 5 midpoint crosses, and the hue alone cannot separate
// those on a 2-pixel column; the readouts can. Nothing about the drawing changed.
//
// WHY A SEPARATE INDICATOR: Pine's `overlay` is fixed at compile time — a single
// script can't toggle between the price chart and a pane at runtime. So the
// "panel mode" is this: add or remove this indicator.
//
// SHARED ENGINE: the block classifier + condition codes are the source-of-truth
// copy in ticker-tape.pine. The regions between the `<<SYNC …>>` markers below are
// GENERATED — do not hand-edit them here. Improve the logic in ticker-tape.pine,
// then run scripts/sync-block-engine.sh to mirror it into this file. That keeps
// the panel's counts identical to the tape's squares, with no drift.
//
// LIVE-ONLY, same as Ticker Tape and Barry's own tool: 1T tick data only exists
// inside the intrabar window and fills on realtime/recent bars — the panel lives
// on recent flow, it can't be back-tested over deep history.
//
// ⚠️ WHY THE PANEL AND THE TAPE CAN DISAGREE — all three are expected, none is a bug
// in the shared classifier (which is byte-identical, enforced by the sync script):
//   1. TICK BUDGET. Both files pass `calc_bars_count`, a budget of PRINTS for the
//      whole request rather than a per-bar window (see the input below). Bars the
//      budget never reached have no tick array and count 0. The two settings must be
//      EQUAL; both default to 200,000.
//   2. THE TAPE'S SQUARES DEFAULT TO THE FORMING BAR ONLY (Keep History = 0). Its
//      per-bar numbers still populate the Data Window across the whole window, so
//      compare against those with the crosshair, not against the squares.
//   3. THE TAPE CAPS ITS SQUARES AT 25 PER BAR (BLOCK_SQUARE_MAX). Its counts stay
//      exact past that; only the drawing stops. A 40-block bar is 25 squares and a
//      column of 40 here — both correct.
// There is no fourth: the tape's volume-delta fallback (an aggregate ≥ threshold
// registering as a "block" on symbols with no live 1T stream) was removed from
// ticker-tape.pine on 2026-08-10. Both files are real-prints-only, so a dead feed
// shows an empty tape AND an empty panel rather than two different approximations.
// Do not reintroduce it on either side.

//@version=6
indicator(title = "Ticker Tape — Block Trades", shorttitle = "🐋 Tape Blocks", overlay = false, max_bars_back = 0, precision = 0)

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 🧱 CONSTANTS
// Shared immutable values used by the indicator, including the Nord palette.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Nord Theme Color
// Polar Night
const color NORD0  = #2E3440
const color NORD1  = #3B4252
const color NORD2  = #434C5E
const color NORD3  = #4C566A
// Snow Storm
const color NORD4  = #D8DEE9
const color NORD5  = #E5E9F0
const color NORD6  = #ECEFF4
// Frost
const color NORD7  = #8FBCBB
const color NORD8  = #88C0D0
const color NORD9  = #81A1C1
const color NORD10 = #5E81AC
// Aurora
const color NORD11 = #BF616A
const color NORD12 = #D08770
const color NORD13 = #EBCB8B
const color NORD14 = #A3BE8C
const color NORD15 = #B48EAD

// Tick-classification condition codes — shared with ticker-tape.pine.
// <<SYNC block-cond BEGIN>> — generated from ticker-tape.pine; run scripts/sync-block-engine.sh
const int COND_MID          = 0
const int COND_AT_ASK       = 1
const int COND_ABOVE_ASK    = 2
const int COND_ABOVE_PRICE  = 3
const int COND_AT_BID       = 4
const int COND_BELOW_BID    = 5
const int COND_BELOW_PRICE  = 6
// <<SYNC block-cond END>>

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 🐋 BLOCK TRADES
// The whole indicator: its inputs, the shared classifier, the per-bar count engine
// and the bar color it exports to the render section. There is only one subsystem
// here, so everything block-related lives in this one section.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
const string GROUP_DATA  = "🐋 Block Trades"
const string GROUP_STYLE = "🎨 Style"

// Barry's sizing rule: tune per market until block spikes land at meaningful spots
// (cycle highs/lows, breakouts). His numbers — futures: ES/YM 20+, E-minis 100 /
// Nasdaq 50. Stocks: 10,000-share bare minimum for ANY stock.
//
// ⚠️ THE 10,000 DOES NOT PORT TO A HIGH-PRICED ETF — it is a GENERIC-stock figure, given
// in the same breath as "it depends on the volume that trades in that market, which can
// be dramatically different from stock to stock… there's no simple answer." His actual
// criterion is the size of the ACCOUNT behind the order: "we're not only capturing the
// institutions here. We're just capturing people who are trading big enough accounts
// with big enough size that the odds are they're probably making money at this point in
// their lives." An amateur-exclusion filter, not a whale detector.
//
// That criterion is what makes his own numbers agree, and they only agree in account
// size — never in notional, because futures run 50-100:1 intraday and equities 4:1:
//   ES 100 / NQ 50 / YM 20 contracts   →  ~$20-150k of day margin
//   10,000 shares of a $50 stock       →  ~$125k at 4:1
//   1,000 shares of QQQ at ~$733       →  ~$188k at 4:1   ← the default, already at the
//   10,000 shares of QQQ at ~$733      →  ~$1.83M           top of his band; 10k here is
//                                                            ~40x past what he meant
// So the 1,000 default is not a placeholder waiting to be raised to Barry's stock floor.
// If you move it, 1,500-2,500 is the defensible direction. And tune it PER SYMBOL from
// the Max Print readout below — never by porting a number off another instrument.
blockSizeThreshold = input.int(defval = 1000, title = "Block Size Threshold", minval = 1, step = 1, tooltip = "Trades at or above this size count as a block on the bar. 1,000 is the tape's institutional floor and keeps this panel populated.\n\nBarry's numbers: futures ES/YM 20+, E-minis 100, Nasdaq 50; stocks a 10,000-share bare minimum. ⚠️ That 10,000 is a GENERIC-stock figure and does NOT port to a high-priced ETF — on QQQ at ~$733 it is ~$7.3M a print (~$1.8M of buying power), roughly 40x the account size his own futures settings imply. The 1,000 default already needs ~$188k at 4:1 intraday, which is at the top of his $20k-150k band.\n\nTune PER SYMBOL, by trial and error, until spikes land at cycle highs/lows and breakouts. Use the 'Max Print (size)' series in the Data Window to see where this symbol's print-size tail actually starts, rather than guessing or porting a number from another instrument.\n\nSet this to match Ticker Tape's own Block Size Threshold if you want the panel and the squares to agree print-for-print.", group = GROUP_DATA)

// ⚠️ THIS NUMBER MUST EQUAL Ticker Tape's own. It is `calc_bars_count`, and it is THE
// budget for the entire request — raise it and you get more tape, full stop.
//
// UNIT = TICKS, verified against TradingView's docs on 2026-08-14 (it shipped as 500
// under a "recent chart bars" reading, which was wrong by ~3 orders of magnitude):
//   · Limitations page: a script may retrieve up to 100K intrabars on Basic/Essential/
//     Plus/Premium, 125K on Expert, 200K on Ultimate, via request.security() or
//     request.security_lower_tf().
//   · Other timeframes and data: "Scripts can retrieve up to 200,000 intrabars from a
//     lower-timeframe context", and "The number of chart bars with available intrabar
//     data varies with the requested lower timeframe, the calc_bars_count value, and
//     the user's plan."
// That second sentence settles it: calc_bars_count is denominated in INTRABARS, and
// the chart-bar coverage is the DERIVED quantity. The context here is "1T", so an
// intrabar is one print. The budget is spent from the newest bar backwards; when it
// runs out, older bars simply have no tick array and count 0 — it undercounts rather
// than lying, but note that a partially-served bar biases the COLOUR as well as the
// height, since both come off the same truncated sample.
//
// 200,000 is the Ultimate ceiling and the plan ceiling applies below that, so this
// default is safe on any account (Premium behaves as 100,000). HENRY'S ACCOUNT IS
// ULTIMATE (confirmed 2026-08-14) — this default is served in full on his charts, so
// there is nothing left to raise. The only reason to lower it is load time.
//
// ⚠️ THE "~1,002 PER-BAR CAP" IS RETRACTED (2026-08-21). It was recorded here and in
// ticker-tape.pine after a 2026-07-29 session where minutes carrying >1,000 trades
// lost 15-20% of their volume against the raw feed. A replay of today's open
// DISPROVES it: the 09:30 bar served 2,765 entries and 09:31 served 1,451, both read
// straight off the Ticks Served probe below. There is no ~1,002 ceiling, and no
// per-bar ceiling has been demonstrated at any number — only a per-bar array whose
// size tracks the minute's traffic.
//
// The 2026-07-29 volume shortfall was real but MISATTRIBUTED. The likeliest cause is
// that TradingView's 1T feed is itself lossy on heavy minutes (consolidation at the
// source), which produces exactly that volume gap with no array limit involved. That
// would make it a DATA-COMPLETENESS limit, which no Pine-side engine change can fix —
// as opposed to a ceiling, which a time-keyed watermark could have worked around.
// Unresolved, and worth keeping in mind before trusting a busy bar's count to the
// share: the panel counts every print it is SERVED, exactly, and cannot know what it
// was not served.
//
// The real, documented constraint is the budget above: 200,000 intrabars TOTAL, spent
// newest-first. At today's rates (~900-1,500 prints/min, 2,765 at the open) that is
// roughly 2-3 hours of 1-min bars before older bars have no array at all.
tickBudget = input.int(defval = 200000, title = "Tick Budget", minval = 1000, maxval = 200000, step = 1000, tooltip = "calc_bars_count for the 1T request — the number of TICKS this indicator asks TradingView for, shared across the whole request rather than allocated per bar. The budget is spent from the newest bar backwards, so it decides how far back the panel has any data at all.\n\nCeilings are per plan: 100,000 on Basic/Essential/Plus/Premium, 125,000 on Expert, 200,000 on Ultimate. Your plan's ceiling applies to anything above it, so the 200,000 default is safe everywhere.\n\nKEEP THIS EQUAL TO Ticker Tape's own Tick Budget, or the two will see different amounts of tape and disagree on exactly the bars only one of them can reach.\n\nLower it if the chart is slow to load — that is the only reason to.", group = GROUP_DATA)

// A block count is a COUNT — never negative, floor always zero. Pine has no
// scale-range API, so the DATA range is pinned by two fully transparent anchor plots
// (0 and this maximum) carried on `display.pane` only: they take part in the pane's
// autoscale but never draw, never reach the Data Window and never reach the status
// line. Zero therefore sits on the pane floor instead of floating wherever autoscale
// puts the smallest bar. Bars taller than the maximum push the top up — the anchors
// set a MINIMUM range, never a clip.
//
// ⚠️ THE ONE THING PINE CANNOT DO: TradingView pads every pane by its Margins setting
// (default ~10% top and bottom). That padding is what puts a stray "-1" or "-2"
// gridline under a zero-floored range, and it is a PANE setting, unreachable from a
// script. Kill it once, permanently: right-click this pane's price scale → Settings…
// → Margins → Bottom = 0%. `minval` below is 5 for the same reason — under that, the
// gridline step drops below 1 and a 0-decimal axis prints duplicate labels.
scaleMaximum = input.int(defval = 10, title = "Scale Maximum", minval = 5, maxval = 100, step = 5, tooltip = "Top of the pane when block activity is quiet. The scale runs 0 → this value with zero pinned to the bottom, so bar heights stay comparable bar to bar instead of rescaling to whatever is on screen. Busier bars that exceed it push the top up; nothing is ever clipped. Raise it on heavy symbols or low thresholds, lower it to magnify a thin tape.\n\nIf you still see a negative gridline below zero, that is TradingView's pane padding, not this script — right-click the price scale → Settings… → Margins → set Bottom to 0%.", group = GROUP_STYLE)

// ── The block palette: TICKER TAPE'S OWN HUES, one input per block type ──
// Cyan (NORD8) is the buy side, Aurora red (NORD11) the sell side — the identical
// pair, under the identical names, that ticker-tape.pine paints its block squares
// with, so a square on the price chart and the column under it read as one system.
//
// Six colours, one plot: this palette IS the per-type breakdown. Alpha carries the
// ranking, hue carries the side:
//   60% (transp 40)  aggressive  — lifted the offer / hit the bid
//   20% (transp 80)  passive     — size resting at the ask / at the bid
//   20% (transp 80)  dark pool   — NORD4, mid prints outnumber BOTH sides
//   10% (transp 90)  no lean     — NORD4, buy and sell exactly cancelling
// Set by Henry on 2026-08-14, replacing the amber dark-pool slot and the 40/65/88
// spread. Dark pool and no-lean now share a hue and sit 10 alpha points apart, which
// is the tightest gap in the pane — they are the two "nothing directional here"
// states, so reading one as the other costs nothing.
blockAggBuyColor  = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD8,  transp = 40), title = "Aggressive Buy",  tooltip = "Bar color when the bar leans buy-side AND the lean is aggressive — blocks printed above the ask (or above the previous price when quotes are missing) at least match the ones resting at the ask. Someone is lifting offers with size. Mirrors Ticker Tape's Aggressive Buy square.",  inline = "sq_buy",  group = GROUP_STYLE)
blockAtAskColor   = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD8,  transp = 80), title = "At Ask",          tooltip = "Bar color when the bar leans buy-side but passively — blocks printed exactly AT the ask outnumber the aggressive ones. Buy-side interest, no urgency. Mirrors Ticker Tape's At Ask square.",  inline = "sq_buy",  group = GROUP_STYLE)
blockAtBidColor   = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD11, transp = 80), title = "At Bid",          tooltip = "Bar color when the bar leans sell-side but passively — blocks printed exactly AT the bid outnumber the aggressive ones. Sell-side interest, no urgency. Mirrors Ticker Tape's At Bid square.", inline = "sq_sell", group = GROUP_STYLE)
blockAggSellColor = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD11, transp = 40), title = "Aggressive Sell", tooltip = "Bar color when the bar leans sell-side AND the lean is aggressive — blocks printed below the bid (or below the previous price when quotes are missing) at least match the ones resting at the bid. Someone is hitting bids with size. Mirrors Ticker Tape's Aggressive Sell square.", inline = "sq_sell", group = GROUP_STYLE)
blockMidColor     = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD4,  transp = 80), title = "Dark Pool (mid-dominant)", tooltip = "Bar color when MID prints outnumber the whole buy side AND the whole sell side — the bar is about size crossing at the midpoint (off-exchange / dark pool), not about a directional lean. Neutral snow-storm white because it belongs to neither side: on a penny-wide symbol there is no on-exchange price between bid and ask, so these are almost always ATS prints.", group = GROUP_STYLE)
blockNeutralColor = input.color(defval = color.new(color = NORD4,  transp = 90), title = "Balanced / No Lean",      tooltip = "Bar color when buy and sell exactly cancel and no mid plurality exists — genuinely nothing to read. The dimmest tier in the pane, one step below the dark-pool bars it shares a hue with.", group = GROUP_STYLE)

// ── THE classifier + side derivation — GENERATED from ticker-tape.pine. Quotes
// are authoritative; tick-to-tick direction is only a no-quote fallback. Because
// this is the same code the tape runs, the panel's counts and net-delta can never
// drift from the squares. Edit in ticker-tape.pine, then run the sync script.
// NOTE: f_tickDirection is carried but not called by the panel's own engine — the
// panel buckets every block by TYPE and derives its net lean from those buckets,
// the same way the tape does. It stays because the region is mirrored verbatim,
// and because any future caller must get the tape's definition, not a local
// reinvention. ──
// <<SYNC block-classify BEGIN>> — generated from ticker-tape.pine; run scripts/sync-block-engine.sh
f_classifyTick(float tickPrice, float tickBid, float tickAsk, float previousPrice) =>
    bool hasValidQuotes = not na(tickBid) and not na(tickAsk) and tickBid > 0 and tickAsk > 0
    int tickCondition = COND_MID
    if hasValidQuotes
        if tickPrice >= tickAsk
            tickCondition := tickPrice == tickAsk ? COND_AT_ASK : COND_ABOVE_ASK
        else if tickPrice <= tickBid
            tickCondition := tickPrice == tickBid ? COND_AT_BID : COND_BELOW_BID
    else
        int syntheticDirection = na(previousPrice) ? 0 : tickPrice > previousPrice ? 1 : tickPrice < previousPrice ? -1 : 0
        tickCondition := syntheticDirection > 0 ? COND_ABOVE_PRICE : syntheticDirection < 0 ? COND_BELOW_PRICE : COND_MID
    tickCondition

// Buy-side / sell-side / neutral, straight off the condition code.
f_tickDirection(int tickCondition) =>
    tickCondition == COND_ABOVE_ASK or tickCondition == COND_AT_ASK or tickCondition == COND_ABOVE_PRICE ? 1 : tickCondition == COND_BELOW_BID or tickCondition == COND_AT_BID or tickCondition == COND_BELOW_PRICE ? -1 : 0
// <<SYNC block-classify END>>

// ── Pull this bar's 1T ticks (same request/order as ticker-tape.pine) ──
// expression order [close, volume, bid, ask] → destructured in the SAME order.
// `time` is deliberately NOT requested: the panel has no rows to timestamp, and
// every field asked for here costs another served series.
[tickPrices, tickVolumes, tickBids, tickAsks] = request.security_lower_tf(
     symbol            = syminfo.tickerid,
     timeframe         = "1T",
     expression        = [close, volume, bid, ask],
     ignore_invalid_symbol = true,
     ignore_invalid_timeframe = true,
     calc_bars_count   = tickBudget)

// The request returns na (NOT empty arrays) on symbols/plans/sessions without tick
// data, so every array has to be proven before it is indexed. Price+volume are the
// two the count can't run without; quotes are optional — the classifier already has
// a no-quote fallback, and a symbol can serve prints with no bid/ask attached.
bool tickBatchValid = not na(tickPrices) and not na(tickVolumes)
int availableTickCount = tickBatchValid ? tickPrices.size() : 0
bool quotesServed = not na(tickBids) and not na(tickAsks)

// ── PER-BAR RECOUNT, watermark-gated (ticker-tape.pine's revised engine) ──
// The count is a pure function of the CURRENT bar's tick array, rebuilt from
// scratch — nothing carried across executions or bars, so a reload, a revised
// tail, or feed lag can never double-count a print.
//
// PERFORMANCE: the naive version re-ran this O(n) scan on EVERY script execution.
// On a realtime bar that is every quote update and every timer tick — hundreds of
// full 1,000-element passes per minute, all of them recomputing an identical
// answer. The tape's fix is a watermark: rescan only on executions where the tick
// array actually changed, and hold the last result in `varip` SCALARS the plot
// reads. Idle executions now do zero work.
//
// varip SCALARS, never UDT fields: object-field `:=` rolls back to the last
// confirmed value on each realtime tick, which is exactly what froze the tape's
// original watermark. Scalars persist.
//
// Where the tape must gate on `>` (its tick pipeline stores incrementally and a
// shrinking array would replay rows), this rescan is fully idempotent, so it gates
// on `!=` — a feed revision that shortens the array resyncs instead of holding a
// stale, too-high count.
//
// ⚠️ BUT NEVER ON AN EMPTY ARRAY, and that guard is not optional. `!=` on its own
// treats "the request served nothing this execution" as a revision down to zero:
// the rescan reruns over 0 ticks, latches every bucket to 0, and the plot commits
// `na` onto a bar that printed blocks. The bar is closed by then — nothing later
// puts the column back. It is exactly the failure ticker-tape.pine defends against
// by gating on `>` (see its varip-scalar note: the LTF array can be EMPTY at the
// bar's confirmed tick, which is the tick the plots commit at), and a transient
// na batch — `tickBatchValid` false — lands in the same place. So the gate carries
// `availableTickCount > 0`: a genuine shrink to 1..n still resyncs, a data-less
// execution HOLDS the last good count. No stale-count risk in the other direction,
// because `barstate.isnew` has already zeroed every bucket, so a bar that truly
// served no prints stays at 0 without the rescan ever running.
//
// The one accepted cost is the tape's: a forming 1T sub-bar
// whose volume revises upward past the threshold WITHOUT appending an index shows
// its prior count until the next print lands (~1s). Self-correcting, and cheap
// next to a full rescan on every quote update.
//
// The buckets below are the tape's, one per block TYPE, because the type breakdown
// is what picks the colour — a bar of five at-ask blocks and a bar of five hit-the-
// bid blocks are the same column height and opposite information. The condition →
// bucket mapping mirrors f_recordBlockTradeState in ticker-tape.pine exactly,
// including the no-quote price fallbacks folding into the aggressive buckets they
// stand in for. It is written inline rather than synced because the tape's version
// is a method on a UDT this panel has no need for; the COND_* codes it switches on
// ARE synced, so a new condition code shows up here as an unhandled case.
varip int blockProcessedTickCount = 0
varip int blockShownCount = 0
varip int blockShownAggBuy = 0
varip int blockShownAtAsk = 0
varip int blockShownAtBid = 0
varip int blockShownAggSell = 0
varip int blockShownMid = 0

// 🔬 CALIBRATION AID — the largest single print on the bar, in shares/contracts,
// measured across EVERY served print rather than only the ones that cleared the
// threshold. Data Window only; it never draws and never touches the pane's scale.
// This is what makes Barry's "play around with it until you start to see the
// meaningful patterns" tunable per symbol instead of guessed: crosshair the bars that
// matter (cycle turns, breakouts, the open) and read where this symbol's print-size
// tail actually begins. The whole question is what the distribution looks like BELOW
// the current cut, which is exactly what the block count cannot show you.
varip float blockShownMaxSize = na

// 🧪 SERVING PROBE — TEMPORARY (2026-08-21, Henry). Rebuilt the same day it was
// written: the first version hunted for an assumed ~1,002-print ceiling, and the
// first replay of the open DISPROVED that ceiling — the 09:30 bar served 2,765
// entries and 09:31 served 1,451. So there is no magic number here any more; this
// version measures what is actually happening with no threshold baked in.
//
//   • Ticks Served (peak) — the largest array this bar ever handed us. If a real
//     per-bar ceiling exists, this is what plateaus at it across many busy bars.
//     If it keeps taking whatever value the minute's traffic implies, there is no
//     ceiling and nothing to work around.
//   • Rescans — how many times the count engine actually rebuilt on this bar. On a
//     LIVE forming bar this must keep climbing while prints arrive; if it stalls
//     while the minute is still running, the count has stopped tracking the tape,
//     which is the failure worth catching.
//   • Tail Changes (no growth) — the array's last element moved while its SIZE did
//     not. That is the signature of a ROLLING window: prints still arriving, the
//     size already at whatever limit exists, so the size-based watermark below goes
//     blind to them. Any non-zero reading means the fix is to key the watermark on
//     print TIME instead of array length. No floor is applied, so this is valid at
//     any ceiling, including none.
//
// Replay caveat: a replayed bar runs ONE execution, so Rescans reads 1 and Tail
// Changes cannot read anything but 0. Both need a live forming bar to mean
// anything. Ticks Served (peak) is valid in replay.
varip int blockPeakTicksServed = 0
varip int blockLastSeenSize = 0
varip int blockRescanCount = 0
varip int blockTailChanges = 0
varip float blockLastTailPrice = na
varip float blockLastTailVolume = na

if barstate.isnew
    blockPeakTicksServed := 0
    blockLastSeenSize := 0
    blockRescanCount := 0
    blockTailChanges := 0
    blockLastTailPrice := na
    blockLastTailVolume := na
    blockProcessedTickCount := 0
    blockShownCount := 0
    blockShownAggBuy := 0
    blockShownAtAsk := 0
    blockShownAtBid := 0
    blockShownAggSell := 0
    blockShownMid := 0
    blockShownMaxSize := na

// Runs on EVERY execution, deliberately outside the watermark gate below — in the
// rolling case that gate is precisely what stops firing, so a probe inside it
// would see nothing and "prove" the array frozen.
if availableTickCount > 0
    if availableTickCount > blockPeakTicksServed
        blockPeakTicksServed := availableTickCount
    float probeTailPrice = tickPrices.get(index = availableTickCount - 1)
    float probeTailVolume = tickVolumes.get(index = availableTickCount - 1)
    // Evidence only when the SIZE held steady and the tail moved anyway. While the
    // array is still growing the tail changes on every execution, which proves
    // nothing. No size floor — a rolling window is a rolling window at any length.
    if availableTickCount == blockLastSeenSize and not na(blockLastTailPrice) and
         (probeTailPrice != blockLastTailPrice or probeTailVolume != blockLastTailVolume)
        blockTailChanges += 1
    blockLastTailPrice := probeTailPrice
    blockLastTailVolume := probeTailVolume
    blockLastSeenSize := availableTickCount

if availableTickCount != blockProcessedTickCount and availableTickCount > 0
    // ⚡ NATIVE PREPASS (2026-09-02, profiler showed this block at 40%+ of runtime).
    // array.max() ignores na elements and returns na only on an empty or all-na
    // array (documented) — exactly what the loop's own per-element accumulation
    // used to compute, so the 🔬 calibration max comes from built-in code instead
    // of a Pine-side op per print. The max then doubles as an EARLY OUT: when the
    // bar's largest print is below the threshold, no print can qualify, every
    // bucket keeps the zero it was declared with, and the O(n) classification loop
    // is skipped entirely — which is MOST executions, since most bars carry no
    // block-size print. Outputs are identical in every case; only the work moved.
    float rebuiltMaxSize = tickVolumes.max()
    int rebuiltCount = 0
    int rebuiltAggBuy = 0
    int rebuiltAtAsk = 0
    int rebuiltAtBid = 0
    int rebuiltAggSell = 0
    int rebuiltMid = 0
    if not na(rebuiltMaxSize) and rebuiltMaxSize >= blockSizeThreshold
        for tickIndex = 0 to availableTickCount - 1
            float tickVolume = tickVolumes.get(index = tickIndex)
            if not na(tickVolume) and tickVolume >= blockSizeThreshold
                // Same classifier as the tape's own rows — a block counted here can
                // never disagree with the square the tape stacks for that print.
                int tickCondition = f_classifyTick(tickPrice = tickPrices.get(index = tickIndex),
                     tickBid = quotesServed ? tickBids.get(index = tickIndex) : na,
                     tickAsk = quotesServed ? tickAsks.get(index = tickIndex) : na,
                     previousPrice = tickIndex > 0 ? tickPrices.get(index = tickIndex - 1) : na)
                rebuiltCount += 1
                if tickCondition == COND_ABOVE_ASK or tickCondition == COND_ABOVE_PRICE
                    rebuiltAggBuy += 1
                else if tickCondition == COND_AT_ASK
                    rebuiltAtAsk += 1
                else if tickCondition == COND_AT_BID
                    rebuiltAtBid += 1
                else if tickCondition == COND_BELOW_BID or tickCondition == COND_BELOW_PRICE
                    rebuiltAggSell += 1
                else
                    rebuiltMid += 1
    // Latch into the varip scalars the render reads. A later data-less execution
    // (quote or timer update) skips the rescan entirely, so these HOLD the last
    // good value and the plot commits it onto the closed bar.
    blockShownCount := rebuiltCount
    blockShownAggBuy := rebuiltAggBuy
    blockShownAtAsk := rebuiltAtAsk
    blockShownAtBid := rebuiltAtBid
    blockShownAggSell := rebuiltAggSell
    blockShownMid := rebuiltMid
    blockShownMaxSize := rebuiltMaxSize
    blockProcessedTickCount := availableTickCount
    blockRescanCount += 1  // 🧪 probe

// Net block delta — the bar's order-flow lean in one number: buy-side (at ask +
// above ask) − sell-side (at bid + below bid), mid prints neutral. Identical
// formula to ticker-tape.pine's, off the same buckets.
int blockBuySide  = blockShownAtAsk + blockShownAggBuy
int blockSellSide = blockShownAtBid + blockShownAggSell
int blockNetDelta = blockBuySide - blockSellSide

// ── DARK POOL GATE: mid prints are invisible to the net delta, so they get their own
// verdict BEFORE the lean is read ──
// The delta above is (buy − sell). Mid prints appear in NEITHER term, so a bar whose
// blocks were all printed at the midpoint nets to exactly zero — identical, to the
// old color logic, to a bar with one buy and one sell. Those are opposite readings
// wearing one color, and on a penny-wide symbol the mid case is the COMMON one:
// with bid and ask a cent apart there is no on-exchange price strictly between them,
// so a mid classification is almost always an OFF-EXCHANGE print — dark pool / ATS
// crossing at the midpoint. Institutions working size without showing it, which is
// the entire reason to watch block prints.
//
// Measured on a live 1-min QQQ pane (2026-08-14, 1,000-share threshold): 28 of the 67
// block-carrying bars painted neutral, and 18 of those 28 held EXACTLY ONE block. One
// block cannot "balance" against itself, so those 18 were real midpoint prints being
// drawn as "nothing happened" — the single most common thing the panel detected was
// also the thing it hid. Worse in the other direction: 5 mid blocks plus 1 at-ask
// netted +1 and painted a buy bar off one print out of six.
//
// The gate: when mid is the PLURALITY bucket — strictly more mid prints than the whole
// buy side and than the whole sell side — the bar is about the dark pool, not about a
// lean. Strictly greater, so a genuine tie (2 mid vs 2 at-ask) still defers to the
// side that has directional information. Everything else falls through to the lean.
bool blockMidDominant = blockShownMid > blockBuySide and blockShownMid > blockSellSide

// ── Per-bar color: SIDE from the net lean, TIER from the aggression on that side ──
// The net delta picks the hue — cyan for a buy-side bar, red for a sell-side bar,
// NORD4 only on an exact balance with no mid plurality. The winning side's own two
// buckets then pick the tier: aggressive prints (through the quote) at least matching
// the passive ones (at the quote) earns the present tier, otherwise the whisper tier.
// Ties go to aggressive — a bar split evenly between lifting offers and resting on the
// ask is the more actionable reading of the two.
//
// The side that LOST contributes nothing to the color beyond having lost; it is
// already in the net delta's magnitude.
color blockBarColor = blockMidDominant ? blockMidColor : blockNetDelta > 0 ? (blockShownAggBuy >= blockShownAtAsk ? blockAggBuyColor : blockAtAskColor) : blockNetDelta < 0 ? (blockShownAggSell >= blockShownAtBid ? blockAggSellColor : blockAtBidColor) : blockNeutralColor

// ── PER-TYPE COUNTS, Data Window only (Henry 2026-08-21) ──
// The five buckets the colour is derived from, exposed as their own series so the
// crosshair reads the breakdown instead of inferring it from one hue: a 5-block bar
// is 5 at-ask, or 5 hit-the-bid, or 5 midpoint crosses, and those are three
// different pieces of information wearing one column. Buy side, sell side and net
// delta are all sums of these five, so nothing further needs its own line.
//
// These are NOT gated to zero-block bars the way the column is — a bar that served
// prints and qualified none is a real, readable 0 across all five. What they ARE
// gated on is whether the bar had TICK DATA AT ALL, and blockShownMaxSize is
// already exactly that latch: it stays na unless at least one print with a real
// volume was served. Without the gate every bar the 1T budget never reached would
// report "0 dark pool blocks, 0 at ask…" — a confident zero standing in for a bar
// the indicator knows nothing about, which is the one thing a readout must never
// do (AI.md: na fallbacks must not impersonate data). na shows as n/a instead.
bool blockTicksServedThisBar = not na(blockShownMaxSize)
float blockTypeAggBuy  = blockTicksServedThisBar ? blockShownAggBuy  : na
float blockTypeAtAsk   = blockTicksServedThisBar ? blockShownAtAsk   : na
float blockTypeAtBid   = blockTicksServedThisBar ? blockShownAtBid   : na
float blockTypeAggSell = blockTicksServedThisBar ? blockShownAggSell : na
float blockTypeMid     = blockTicksServedThisBar ? blockShownMid     : na

// Readout COLOUR, prepared here with the series it belongs to (AI.md plotting rule:
// only the final plot() statement lives in the render section).
//
// COLOUR IS THE SIDE, THE LABEL IS THE TIER — and that split is forced by the colour
// system, not a preference. The four directional inputs encode side as HUE and
// aggression as ALPHA (NORD8 t40/t80 buy, NORD11 t80/t40 sell). Text at transp 80 is
// illegible on the dark background, but forcing all four to transp 0 collapses the
// alpha channel and renders AggBuy identical to AtAsk and AtBid identical to AggSell
// — four labels, two colours, the tier silently gone. (Shipped that way on
// 2026-08-21; corrected 2026-08-22.) Nord has no second red to break the tie with
// either. So the readout takes the SIDE hue at full opacity and lets the label carry
// the tier, which it already does unambiguously: "Aggressive Buy" against "At Ask".
color blockSideBuyText  = color.new(color = blockAggBuyColor,  transp = 0)
color blockSideSellText = color.new(color = blockAggSellColor, transp = 0)
color blockSideMidText  = color.new(color = blockMidColor,     transp = 0)

// 🚫 THE ZERO LINE: a bar with no blocks is a ZERO, and a bar-style plot fed 0
// still draws — a zero-HEIGHT bar, which TradingView renders as a stub sitting on
// the baseline. Across the long stretches with no block prints (and all the history
// 1T data never reaches) that stub repeats bar after bar and reads as a solid line
// ruled at zero. It was never an hline; it is the plot drawing its own zeros. `na`
// makes those bars draw NOTHING and leaves the baseline bare.
// Consequence to know about: restyle this to Line or Area in the Style tab and the
// trace will BREAK at every blockless bar rather than running along zero. That is
// the trade for a clean baseline — the count is event data, not a continuous series.
float blockShownBlocks = blockShownCount > 0 ? blockShownCount : na

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// 🎨 RENDER
// Final plot statements only — every series, colour and visibility flag they read is
// computed in the section above.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// THE plot — the only one that draws, the only one in the Data Window and the status
// line. Height is the block count, colour is everything else the bar knows, and both
// are recomputed intrabar off the current tick array.
//
// No "Plot Type" input: the Style tab already carries TradingView's own type dropdown
// (Histogram / Columns / Line / Area / Step Line / Cross / Circles), so the histogram
// here is a default, not a lock-in.
//
// linewidth IS THE COLUMN WIDTH on a histogram — not a line thickness. 2 renders 2
// device pixels per column on a 2× display against a ~13.5-pixel slot per 1-min bar,
// so the columns read as thin ticks with clear air between them. The tradeoff to know
// about: at this width the COLOUR is a narrow sliver, and the colour is where the
// side/aggression breakdown lives — zoom in when you need to read it, or raise this to
// 4 (TradingView's maximum, ~60% slot fill) if you would rather trade crowding for
// legibility. Set to 2 by Henry on 2026-08-15.
plot(series = blockShownBlocks, title = "Blocks", style = plot.style_histogram, color = blockBarColor, linewidth = 2, precision = 0, display = display.pane + display.data_window + display.status_line)

// Scale anchors — see the Scale Maximum input. These draw nothing, are not editable
// and are not readable anywhere; they exist only so the pane's autoscale runs
// 0 → Scale Maximum with zero on the floor. Bar styles base themselves at zero
// natively, so the FLOOR anchor is what keeps zero down there after a Style-tab
// switch to Line; the CEILING anchor is what stops a 1-block bar filling the pane.
plot(series = 0,            title = "Scale Floor",   color = color.new(color = NORD3, transp = 100), display = display.pane, editable = false)
plot(series = scaleMaximum, title = "Scale Ceiling", color = color.new(color = NORD3, transp = 100), display = display.pane, editable = false)

// 📊 PER-TYPE BREAKDOWN — status line + Data Window (Henry 2026-08-21: readable on
// hover, not only under the crosshair). NOT on `display.pane`, so none of these
// draws a column and none joins the autoscale — the histogram is untouched.
//
// Ordered as a ladder, most-buy at the top down to most-sell, with the mid bucket
// last: Aggressive Buy → At Ask → At Bid → Aggressive Sell → Dark Pool / Mid. Read
// top to bottom that is the bar's flow. Titles are spelled out in full — an
// abbreviated set was tried on 2026-08-21 and reverted the next day; the status line
// truncates gracefully on its own and the Data Window has the room.
//
// Colours (`blockType*Text`) are prepared in the owning section above.
plot(series = blockTypeAggBuy,  title = "Aggressive Buy (blocks)",  color = blockSideBuyText,  display = display.status_line + display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockTypeAtAsk,   title = "At Ask (blocks)",           color = blockSideBuyText,  display = display.status_line + display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockTypeAtBid,   title = "At Bid (blocks)",           color = blockSideSellText, display = display.status_line + display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockTypeAggSell, title = "Aggressive Sell (blocks)",  color = blockSideSellText, display = display.status_line + display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockTypeMid,     title = "Dark Pool / Mid (blocks)",  color = blockSideMidText,  display = display.status_line + display.data_window, editable = false)

// 🧪 CAP PROBE READOUT — Data Window ONLY, temporary. See the probe block above:
// crosshair a busy minute, and if "Array Pegged" reads 1 then "Tail Changes"
// decides whether the lost prints are recoverable in Pine at all.
plot(series = blockPeakTicksServed, title = "Ticks Served (peak)",        display = display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockRescanCount,     title = "Rescans",                   display = display.data_window, editable = false)
plot(series = blockTailChanges,     title = "Tail Changes (no growth)",  display = display.data_window, editable = false)

// 🔬 CALIBRATION READOUT — Data Window ONLY. Not on the pane, not in the status line,
// not in the autoscale, so it cannot disturb the columns. See the varip declaration
// above: this is the evidence you set Block Size Threshold from, per symbol.
plot(series = blockShownMaxSize, title = "Max Print (size)", display = display.data_window, editable = false)
